<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=quern-stone">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quern-stone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 9000 BCE</span> · Also known as saddle quern, rotary quern</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A quern-stone is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tool</a> for hand-grinding grain and other materials, used in pairs: a lower stationary stone and an upper stone moved across it. The earliest quern so far discovered, found at Abu Hureyra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, dates to c. 9000 BCE, and the tool was first used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> to grind cereals into flour. The saddle quern, worked back and forth, was the most ancient and widely used type; the rotary quern, with a central eye and hopper, appeared later and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. Querns were generally replaced by millstones once water mills and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmills</a> appeared, though in many places they remained in use until the last century or so.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quern-stone.jpg" alt="Quern-stone" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Davidbena · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basalt_quern.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 9000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Abu Hureyra, Syria</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Grinding stones for vegetal food, and possibly flour, were widespread across Europe from at least 30,000 years ago, and a prehistoric quern from Longwangchan in Shaanxi, China, is dated to 23,000 BCE, earlier than the Abu Hureyra find.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools<span>3.3 million years ago</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=quern-stone">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
